  "summary": "A style blending Dutch architectural elements with local materials and techniques in colonial contexts.",
  "description": "Characterized by symmetrical facades, steep roofs, and verandas adapted to tropical climates, it reflects Dutch practicality combined with local building traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Dutch Colonial architecture — from the Hudson Valley's gambrel-roofed farmhouses (1730s) to Batavia's canals, Cape Town's warehouses and Curaçao's Willemstad gables — carried Dutch practicality worldwide: brick walls, stepped or neck gables, deep eaves, and the American gambrel that gave \"Dutch Colonial Revival\" (1890s-1930) its barn-like suburban coziness. New Amsterdam street plans survive in lower Manhattan; Willemstad's Handelskade is UNESCO-listed Caribbean Dutch.",
